# PO specify - structure one raw feature page

<!-- prism:po-specify-contract:v1 -->

## Usage

`/po-specify [F-XXX]`

## Supported action

This command reads one raw feature page, prepares a canonical structured
specification draft, and, after explicit confirmation, writes one feature-only
transition:

`raw` + `po` -> `specified` + `po`

Resolve exactly one existing source file at
`knowledge/wiki/features/[F-XXX]-[slug].md`. A missing, invalid, or ambiguous
ID is a stop condition. This action does not process or move any intake folder.
It preserves the existing advisory state, sources, apps, and valid tracked
questions, including open PO questions.

Raw input is an authoring source. If it already has the canonical structured
sections and substantive values required for `specified`, verify and preserve
that body; otherwise author the missing structure from supported facts. In
either case, show the body and metadata in the preview. If the body is already
valid, preserve it and show that no body rewrite is proposed; only the confirmed
status/metadata, index, and log updates then remain. Never perform an unverified
status-only write.

## Read-only preflight and source freshness

Read `knowledge/wiki/SCHEMA.md`, `knowledge/wiki/LIFECYCLE.md`, `knowledge/wiki/index.md`, the complete feature,
linked context, and workspace identity before preparing a preview. When installed,
probe the optional CLI:

```text
prism --version
prism wiki transition-preflight F-XXX [path] --action po-specify --json
```

Use that response only when it identifies common envelope schema 1, command facts,
capability version 2 with an action-specific `po-specify` surface, transition
version 1, and a consistent snapshot. The version string alone does not prove
support. If the probe is missing, old, unsupported, or fails, state the direct-file
fallback and follow `LIFECYCLE.md`. The preflight is copy-only and never authorizes a
write.

Before using copied facts, reread this command, the Codex skill when available,
SCHEMA, LIFECYCLE, index, feature, linked context, and identity. Compare the unique path,
identity, status, owner, advisory, and fingerprint. A mismatch or unavailable
comparison requires a fresh preview.

## What this command does

1. Enforce the exact source guard: `status: raw` and `owner: po`.
2. Read the raw body and prepare a proposed canonical structured body with
   `## Summary`, `## User story`, `## Acceptance criteria`, `## App scope`,
   `## Open questions`, `## Design`, `## Related features`, `## API surface`,
   `## Board review summary`, and `## Post-ship notes`. Carry forward supported
   facts from the raw page and linked context. Keep unknowns as explicit owned
   questions for `po`, `designer`, or `dev`; do not invent substance. `TODO`,
   `TBD`, template filler, and empty placeholder requirements are invalid.
   A raw page written by `po-intake` already carries Summary, User story, Acceptance
   criteria, Open questions, and App scope, and leaves Design, Related features,
   API surface, Board review summary, and Post-ship notes empty. Complete those five
   with one line each: supported content, or an explicit statement that nothing exists
   yet (for example `Not started.`, `None identified.`, `None.` under API surface,
   `Not reviewed yet.`, `Not shipped yet.`). Any API surface text other than `None.`
   declares API work and needs an API contract page before `/dev-start`, so write `None.`
   unless the intake material or an answered question states an API change. Keep open
   questions in the Open questions table, never in these five sections. The connected
   board rejects an empty required section with `required_section_missing` and names
   the section.
3. If the raw page already satisfies the complete structured-output gate, the
   proposed body may preserve it unchanged after verification. Otherwise fill
   the missing required sections from supported facts and explicit owned
   questions. Do not invent substance or placeholder requirements. If the raw
   body is already valid, preserve it instead of rewriting it.
4. Validate the proposed frontmatter and body, including a nonempty app
   scope matching the feature's declared apps. Open tracked questions are
   allowed at `specified`.
5. Preserve the current `advisory-review` value. A pending review remains a
   blocker for later actions that require advisory completion.
6. Show the observed fields, complete body diff, metadata diff, destination, and
   every feature, index, and log write that would occur. This preview is not
   approval.
7. Reread all sources immediately before confirmation and once again before
   writing. Ask the user to confirm the complete write set, including the body.
8. After confirmation, write the confirmed body and metadata, update the feature
   to `specified` with `owner: po`, update the matching index row, and append the log
   entry in the log format that the wiki schema defines. Feature pages carry no date field; do not add
   one. Do not move intake folders or write unrelated files.

If the user declines or cancels, make no feature, index, or log mutation. If a
multi-file write is partially applied, report the exact observed changes and stop
for fresh recovery; do not claim a transaction. This action does not prove
semantic completeness, design, implementation, testing, or shipment.

## Page rules

- write current-state pages: replace superseded content in place, state rationale as a current fact and keep history in `log.md` and the records; mark each claim `**Decided:**`, `**Observed:**`, `**Proposed:**` or `**Assumed:**` (gaps stay in the Open questions table) and link the evidence of every Decided and Observed claim: the processed intake item, a record or a URL (see Evidence labels in the wiki schema)
